{"name":"Artillery","hex":"#746f67","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"artillery","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Large projectile weapons, in modern usage usually large guns, but also rocket artillery.","An army unit that uses such weapons, or a military formation using projectile weapons, such as archers."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/artillery"}],"entries":[{"title":"Artillery","blocks":[{"text":"Artillery consists of ranged weapons that launch munitions far beyond the range and power of infantry firearms. Early artillery development focused on the ability to breach defensive walls and fortifications during sieges, and led to heavy, fairly immobile siege engines. As technology improved, lighter, more mobile field artillery cannons were developed for battlefield use. This development continues today; modern self-propelled artillery vehicles are highly mobile weapons of great versatility generally providing the largest share of an army's total firepower."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/French_soldiers_in_the_Franco-Prussian_War_1870-71.jpg/330px-French_soldiers_in_the_Franco-Prussian_War_1870-71.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artillery"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Gari-Melchers-War-Highsmith.jpeg/500px-Gari-Melchers-War-Highsmith.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Gari-Melchers-War-Highsmith.jpeg/1280px-Gari-Melchers-War-Highsmith.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Gari-Melchers-War-Highsmith.jpeg/500px-Gari-Melchers-War-Highsmith.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGari-Melchers-War-Highsmith.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/British_64_Pounder_Rifled_Muzzle-Loaded_%28RML%29_Gun_on_Moncrieff_disappearing_mount%2C_at_Scaur_Hill_Fort%2C_Bermuda.jpg/500px-British_64_Pounder_Rifled_Muzzle-Loaded_%28RML%29_Gun_on_Moncrieff_disappearing_mount%2C_at_Scaur_Hill_Fort%2C_Bermuda.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/British_64_Pounder_Rifled_Muzzle-Loaded_%28RML%29_Gun_on_Moncrieff_disappearing_mount%2C_at_Scaur_Hill_Fort%2C_Bermuda.jpg/500px-British_64_Pounder_Rifled_Muzzle-Loaded_%28RML%29_Gun_on_Moncrieff_disappearing_mount%2C_at_Scaur_Hill_Fort%2C_Bermuda.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"British 64 Pounder Rifled Muzzle-Loaded (RML) Gun on a Moncrieff disappearing mount, at Scaur Hill Fort, Bermuda. This is a part of a fixed battery, meant to protect against over-land attack and to serve as coastal artillery.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABritish_64_Pounder_Rifled_Muzzle-Loaded_(RML)_Gun_on_Moncrieff_disappearing_mount%2C_at_Scaur_Hill_Fort%2C_Bermuda.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/M777_Light_Towed_Howitzer_1.jpg/500px-M777_Light_Towed_Howitzer_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/M777_Light_Towed_Howitzer_1.jpg/1280px-M777_Light_Towed_Howitzer_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/M777_Light_Towed_Howitzer_1.jpg/500px-M777_Light_Towed_Howitzer_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"7-person gun crew firing a US M777 Light Towed Howitzer, War in Afghanistan, 2009","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AM777_Light_